 it I want to be like them I thought it  was fascinating to watch a story about  young dudes who've watched too many  movies and thought they could be these  guys this director primarily has a  documentary background so about twenty  to thirty percent of this movie is  actually cutting back and forth between  interviews with the real for dudes who  pulled off this heist part of the unique  narrative with this storytelling is  there's often conflicting point of views  often over my new details like very  little things like what  shirt was this guy wearing that they met  with but I thought it was a fascinating  narrative because so many times you see  movies that are based on a true story  and you end up questioning and this  actually happened and this actually  happened and this actually happened I  thought it was fascinating for the movie  itself to have conflicting interviews  present both interviews for the movie  itself to be questioning did this  actually happen to this part actually  have I think in the first act it gets a  little too hyper stylized with showing  what they're trying to do with the  narrative with the conflicting  perspectives from interviewing the four  people have different versions of  certain events oh let's have Evan Peters  break away from the scene for a second  and start talking to the actual dude who  he's portraying this stuff like that  where I thought it was a little too much  a little too flashy at first I thought I  probably wouldn't be able to fully get  invested into this film because I  thought its style was a little too in my  face but after a while the style blends  very seamlessly that when it's cutting  back and forth from the interviews and  then the reenactment you're just  invested in both that's going on I think  the direction of this film is really  cool especially in the tone that the  director struck with the film because  it's not one of those movies like in  Ocean's eleven where it's just a giant  build up to a heist and then after the  heist has done the movie ends the movie  kind of has two halves everything before  the heist  then the heist and everything after that  because everything leading up to the  heist is really seeing it from the  perspective of these dudes who really  believe they can pull this heist off  these are just young men who are doing  this so in the style that the director  was choosing to capture the reenactment  its captured in a way as if you're  watching a really bitchin heist movie  and you think these guys are gonna pull  off some crazy cool heist and then the  last half is when it gets real it gets  super realistic like when they get to  the actual heist it's not some cool  badass thing it's scary it's  nerve-racking you feel like pacing the  room because of how like oh my god I  thought it was so cool to see a movie  that is essentially telling you life  isn't a movie just because you saw some  cool stuff go down in a film doesn't  mean you can pull it off in real life  and it's also more than just fulfilling  fantasies scene from a movie these guys  really just hate the idea of conforming  to what society wants them to be like so  the first half cool badass plotting of a  heist movie and then the last half hits  you with a large dose of somber  realism but it works perfectly and the  cast of the four dudes are great the  four dudes have pull off this heist  they're all great actors and they do the  fantastic job and have unique  personalities here but the main actor to  talk about is the lead of this movie and  the guy who leads this whole heist Evan  Peters as Warren Lipka who's the main  guy who brought his buddies together  I've seen most of American Horror Story  and I've said for a long time that I  think Evan Peters is gonna be the next  Johnny Depp not Johnny Depp today like  oh my god a word about giant duck young  Johnny Depp there's something about the  not giving a [ __ ] personality that he  has and the delivery and his ability to  embody of a wide variety of roles that  really reminds me of a young Johnny Depp  his performance here showcases that this  dude can lead the hell out of a movie  he's an eccentric character a dude who  when he's convinced he's good at  convincing other people of what he's  convinced himself up even though this is  a crazy idea this dude is a guy who does  not seem like he's headed down a good  path you can see why he was able to  gather people smarter than himself and  get them believing that they all could  pull off this crazy heist  these four young men who don't want to  conform to the norms of society want to  live in this fantasy world and have the  easy way out you come to learn that life  isn't a movie I really think you guys  should go watch this film it's a  fascinating true story with a unique  narrative an awesome soundtrack by the  way with some great performances in it  and it has the veil of a cool heist  movie but it's a film that's much deeper  than that it's a film that's about  choices it's a film about consequences  and about how fulfilling certain  fantasies does not equate to having a  good reality I was seriously wildly  impressed with the film it's very  stylish it's very well acted very well  directed for a film with this kind of  pacing and great dark comedy I was  surprised by how much deeper it actually  is I would agree this on a scale out of  10 I would give it like an 8.5 or a 9  out of 10 it is seriously one of my  favourite movies of the year it's a film  that I saw a week and a half ago and I  thought about it every day since it's a  damn good movie Evan Peters kills it and  I hope that when awards season comes  around he is considered for Best leading  Actor  all right guys why don't you comment  below and tell me what is your favorite  heist movie you guys can subscribe to  the reject nation click that  notification bell and you can check us  on patreon full-length TV show reactions  weekly Q&amp;A s but loved  have you become a pa